-- Demandé par Tom le 28/10/2026 : version anglaise de l'article Mbappé et la Chine (pour le visuel et la légende en anglais).
UPDATE "articles" SET "en_title" = 'Mbappé and China: how a meme sparked a storm', "en_dek" = 'A throwaway line, an internet edit and a country of 1.4 billion people that feels insulted. In 48 hours, ⭐ Kylian Mbappé lost sponsors and La Liga lost its place on Chinese TV. Passe D explains everything, from start to finish.', "en_body_html" = '<h2>It all started with a meme</h2><p>It began with an innocent question. In a clip linked to the French show The Bridge, ⭐ Kylian Mbappé is asked which meme of himself he likes best. He picks an edit where his face is pasted onto a man he describes as “some guy, I think he''s a Chinese dictator”. That man is Mao Zedong.</p><p>To him, it was an internet joke. In China, it was something else. The video went viral from Monday 26 October, the very evening he finished behind ⭐ Harry Kane in the Ballon d''Or. The anger built very quickly.</p><h2>Who was Mao Zedong?</h2><p>To understand China''s reaction, you need to know who this man was and what he stands for.</p><p>Mao Zedong was born in 1893 in a village in Hunan province, into a peasant family. In 1921, he took part in founding the Chinese Communist Party. After decades of civil war and fighting the Japanese occupation, he proclaimed the People''s Republic of China on 1 October 1949. He led the country until his death in 1976.</p><p>For many Chinese people, he is the founding father of modern China: the man who reunified a country torn apart by wars and foreign humiliation. His portrait still hangs over the gate of Tiananmen Square in Beijing, and his face is on the banknotes.</p><p>In China, his image touches on national sentiment. Mocking Mao, especially as a foreign star, is seen as disrespect towards an entire people and its history.</p><h2>The domino effect</h2><p>The reaction was immediate:</p><ul><li>Three Chinese sponsors dropped him: drinks group Dongpeng, dairy giant Mengniu and Milkground. They cited “zero tolerance” for anything that hurts national sentiment.</li><li>State TV suspended La Liga: China Media Group, the parent company of CCTV, suspended broadcasts of La Liga matches as well as Mbappé-related ads and content. It made apologies from La Liga, the player and Real Madrid a condition for resuming talks.</li><li>China''s foreign ministry publicly criticised his remarks.</li><li>The Chinese Football Association demanded apologies from Mbappé and Real Madrid.</li><li>Video games followed: in China, FC Online removed Mbappé promotional content. The Chinese versions of PES announced they were ending all promotion of him and removing his cards and character.</li><li>On Chinese social media, his Xiaohongshu account was reportedly suspended.</li></ul><h2>The apologies</h2><p>On Tuesday, Mbappé released a statement in French, English and Mandarin. He admitted his comment was “ill-judged”, said he was “sincerely sorry” and stressed his deep respect for the Chinese people and their history.</p><p>La Liga also apologised. In a letter to China Media Group, its president Javier Tebas called the behaviour “unacceptable” and said it did not represent the values of the competition or of Spanish football. On the Chinese side, China Media Group warned that words matter, but actions matter just as much.</p><h2>What impact for Mbappé and football?</h2><p>For ⭐ Kylian Mbappé, the blow is above all financial and reputational. According to Forbes, he is set to earn around $100 million this season, $30 million of it from sponsors and business interests. The Chinese brands did not disclose the value of their contracts, but China had become a key market for him, with huge visibility since the World Cup.</p><p>For Real Madrid and La Liga, the stakes are even higher. China has millions of fans of European football, and TV rights and Asian tours bring in big money. In 2024, La Liga signed its first deal with China Media Group in nine years. That contract is now under strain.</p><p>It is not the first time. In 2019, Chinese TV pulled an Arsenal match after comments by Mesut Özil, and cut the NBA after a tweet by a Houston Rockets executive. In both cases, it took months to ease tensions.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><p>A joke lasting a few seconds was enough to create a crisis. The affair is a reminder of a reality of modern football: a global player speaks to the whole planet, and every word counts. For Mbappé, what happens next now depends on his actions. ⚽</p><h2>Sources</h2><p><a href="https://www.forbes.com/sites/samleveridge/2026/10/27/kylian-mbapp-loses-chinese-sponsors-and-laliga-loses-tv-coverage-after-meme-remark/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Forbes</a>, <a href="https://uk.sports.yahoo.com/news/mbappe-dropped-chinese-brands-offending-040709915.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AFP / Yahoo Sports</a>, <a href="https://www.globaltimes.cn/page/202610/1373080.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Global Times</a>, <a href="https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/28/china-gives-kylian-mbappe-red-card-after-mao-dictator-quip/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1News</a> and <a href="https://www.goal.com/en/news/china-s-storm-of-anger-continues-to-batter-kylian-instant-punishment-expels-mbappe-from-the-virtual-world/bltdcfe4e052282cc9b" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Goal</a>.</p><p>— Passe D</p>', "en_published" = true
WHERE "slug" = 'mbappe-et-la-chine-comment-un-meme-a-declenche-une-tempete' AND "en_title" = '';
